<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antoine Lavoisier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1743">1743</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Antoine-Laurent de Lavoisier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoine Lavoisier was a French nobleman and chemist central to the 18th-century chemical revolution and made chemistry quantitative. He showed the role of oxygen in combustion against the phlogiston theory, named oxygen in 1778 and recognised hydrogen as an element in 1783, and his precise measurements confirmed the conservation of mass. He wrote the first extensive list of elements, helped reform chemical nomenclature and construct the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metric-system">metric system</a> with his wife Marie-Anne Paulze Lavoisier and set out his chemistry in the Traité élémentaire de chimie (1789). An administrator of the Ferme générale, he was charged with tax fraud during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and guillotined in 1794.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoine-lavoisier.jpg" alt="Antoine Lavoisier" width="330" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques-Louis David · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_-_Portrait_of_Monsieur_Lavoisier_(cropped)2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Lavoisier was born to a wealthy noble family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the son of an attorney at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>. He studied law, receiving a licentiate in 1764, but never practised and continued his scientific education in his spare time. He read his first paper to the French Academy of Sciences in 1764, on gypsum, and in 1766 received a gold medal from the King for an essay on urban street lighting.</p><p>Around the time of his election to the Academy he bought a share in the Ferme générale, a tax farm. In 1775 he became one of four commissioners of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, which brought him a house and laboratory at the Royal Arsenal, where he lived and worked from 1775 to 1792. He also served as Secretary of the Royal Commission on Agriculture and spent his own money on improving yields in the Sologne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1774 he weighed the reactants and products of a reaction in a sealed glass vessel and showed that the total mass is unchanged. In 1778 he coined the name oxygen, from Greek words meaning &quot;acid former&quot;. In 1783 he read his Réflexions sur le phlogistique, an attack on the phlogiston theory. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-simon-laplace">Pierre-Simon Laplace</a> he designed an ice calorimeter and concluded from experiments with a guinea pig that respiration is a slow combustion.</p><p>The Méthode de nomenclature chimique of 1787, written with Guyton de Morveau, Claude-Louis Berthollet and Antoine François de Fourcroy, discarded the classical elements and provisionally listed some 33 substances as elements. His Traité élémentaire de chimie of 1789 can be considered the first modern textbook of chemistry. British chemists who held to phlogiston, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-priestley">Joseph Priestley</a> and Richard Kirwan, opposed him, arguing that precise measurement did not imply conservation of mass.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Ferme générale was abolished in March 1791, and in 1792 Lavoisier was forced to resign from the Gunpowder Commission and leave the Royal Arsenal. Charged with tax fraud and selling adulterated tobacco, he was guillotined on 8 May 1794 despite appeals to spare him in recognition of his contributions to science. A year and a half later the French government exonerated him.</p><p>His wife and laboratory assistant, Marie-Anne Paulze Lavoisier, became a chemist in her own right. In 1999 his work was recognised as an International Historic Chemical Landmark by the American Chemical Society, the Académie des sciences and the Société Chimique de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antoine Lavoisier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
